For&nbsp;today's topic I want to talk about 
why you should consider having

00:00:15.486 --> 00:00:18.593
API management in your architecture.

00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:20.974
Hello, my name is Jamil Spain.

00:00:20.974 --> 00:00:24.610
I'm a brand technical specialist &nbsp;
for the Cloud Pak for Integration

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.294
in the U.S. Financial Services market, 

00:00:27.294 --> 00:00:32.317
and when it comes to API management,&nbsp;
I want to focus on three quick areas

00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:35.605
that are really my top three when I talk about this topic.

00:00:35.605 --> 00:00:39.216
One is&nbsp;injecting some flexibility in your architecture.

00:00:39.216 --> 00:00:40.944
The second is security.

00:00:40.944 --> 00:00:45.000
And, more importantly,&nbsp;the analytics 
that you're going to get in return,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.902
So, I ll explain more here.

00:00:46.902 --> 00:00:50.217
Now, before we get&nbsp;started, 
I do want to talk and level set 

00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:54.667
on some common terms that come up  &nbsp;
when we talk about API development.

00:00:54.815 --> 00:00:59.033
Now, there are two major types    
that I like to classify APIs.

00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:04.002
The first is service&nbsp;APIs.

00:01:04.491 --> 00:01:11.013
Now, I consider service APIs anything that  
directly talks to a system of record.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.480
Whether you're an enterprise developer or a

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.116
general startup developer, you're making &nbsp;
your own project here,

00:01:18.116 --> 00:01:21.339
you always want to protect these APIs. 

00:01:21.339 --> 00:01:24.275
These are generally your strongest assets&nbsp;here.

00:01:24.275 --> 00:01:30.212
The second type are interaction APIs.

00:01:31.343 --> 00:01:38.560
Now actually, anything that's not a service&nbsp;
API, I consider to be an interaction API.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:45.040
When I start a project out, I generally start with&nbsp;
my service APIs, and then I start development on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:51.360
these interaction APIs. They may actually call&nbsp;
these service APIs , but let's take a look at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:58.240
word. There's one key piece here. That root of&nbsp;
that is interact , so it is generally making some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.743
interaction on top to facilitate some type of&nbsp; 
operation that you want to programmatically&nbsp;do here.

00:02:04.743 --> 00:02:10.667
This is what starts us off at our first&nbsp;
point here: flexibility in the architecture.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:18.640
Now,  you have consumers who are going to be using&nbsp;
your APIs. In API management, one major concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:24.560
is having an API gateway that you have. So,&nbsp;
your users, consumers of your APIs, will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.843
always interact with the API gateway when they&nbsp;
want to consume your APIs.

00:02:29.843 --> 00:02:34.385
Now, the job of the actual API gateway

00:02:34.385 --> 00:02:40.154
is to perform the interaction&nbsp;layer, 
API layer, there for the most purposes.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.093
Now this is greatly differs if you don't have&nbsp;
API management.

00:02:44.093 --> 00:02:49.630
Users, consumers of your APIs, may directly  
be talking directly to your APIs,

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.545
but&nbsp;this puts a layer in front.

00:02:51.545 --> 00:02:52.720
One of the first things

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.432
that gives you the greatest flexibility&nbsp;
is I get to standardize on an interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:04.960
you know, denoted and documented through open API&nbsp;
swagger specs of course, through those YAMLs there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:11.484
and published in a developer portal so they can&nbsp;easily 
self-service and get on board to using&nbsp;the APIs, 

00:03:11.484 --> 00:03:17.036
but the most important thing is, they&nbsp;define, 
you know, they're going to get a standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.052
definition of these APIs.

00:03:19.052 --> 00:03:22.491
Now, what actually&nbsp;happens on the other side

00:03:22.817 --> 00:03:27.840
for your services that you're running, and I'm &nbsp;
going to do these little database icons here to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:35.680
imitate the API that you're writing here, it could&nbsp;
actually go to one service or it could be multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.040
interactions, invocations to these APIs, and&nbsp;
more of a I've actually done some scatter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.880
gather patterns where I want to hit three services&nbsp;
and build a response that goes back to the user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.238
the consumer.

00:03:48.238 --> 00:03:49.867
Why is that important?

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:53.318
Well, we&nbsp;all know that with 
all those technologies that

00:03:53.318 --> 00:03:54.786
I mentioned at the beginning,

00:03:54.786 --> 00:03:58.607
we want to always&nbsp;be in a place
that we can feel free to innovate,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.759
refactor, explore new technologies.

00:04:01.759 --> 00:04:04.571
Especially&nbsp;in this day and time of container architecture

00:04:04.571 --> 00:04:11.330
and cloud native, it is very easy to now free&nbsp;yourself 
and try many, many new technologies here&nbsp;&nbsp;as well.

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:16.735
If you're providing a singular URL to&nbsp;
your users that, without API management, you have

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:23.280
to always be cognizant that, "Hey, I have to always&nbsp;
be able to update my users to the newest thing."
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.669
Now, whether that's your new latest startup,&nbsp;
or your new latest enterprise application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.794 --> 00:04:33.079
that may be very critical very hard to do in some&nbsp;
perspectives here.

00:04:33.079 --> 00:04:37.756
But by having API management in, it gives me the flexibility

00:04:37.756 --> 00:04:39.665
to do my own&nbsp;innovation inside.

00:04:39.665 --> 00:04:41.259
The interface that I, 

00:04:41.731 --> 00:04:49.520
that I give to my users externally doesn't &nbsp;
always have to directly mirror the architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.295
that I have inside, so I m free to innovate&nbsp;
there as well.

00:04:53.295 --> 00:04:55.849
Second, while this occurs,

00:04:55.849 --> 00:04:59.748
I have an opportunity to implement some security&nbsp;here.

00:04:59.748 --> 00:05:02.652
Each consumer of my APIs when they come up

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:08.800
APIs are generally part of the API management&nbsp;
solution is the APIs have to be published in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:15.804
some type of usually a self-service developer&nbsp;
portal, and they're going to get assigned a client&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.873 --> 00:05:19.736
ID and secret.

00:05:19.736 --> 00:05:25.505
This is a great way of&nbsp;identifying and 
designating that I know

00:05:25.505 --> 00:05:30.649
who is registering their applications and who's&nbsp;
using the APIs there as well.

00:05:30.649 --> 00:05:32.240
On top of that,

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:39.837
the second most important thing with security, is&nbsp;
I get to implement an SLA with rate limiting.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:52.187
Now, this is very, very, very critical because&nbsp;
it gives us a way to kind of conserve the APIs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.187 --> 00:05:54.486
the usage of the APIs, that are coming.

00:05:54.486 --> 00:05:58.853
I can leverage it to make a service level agreement here

00:05:58.853 --> 00:06:03.600
 on how much you can use, or if&nbsp;
you want to actually get into monetization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.850
getting higher tiers, publish a freemium tier, and&nbsp;
then have more higher tiers that you can do. 
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.948 --> 00:06:16.907
You can all facilitate this. This is usually built&nbsp;
into every API management solution. 
&nbsp;

00:06:17.025 --> 00:06:22.100
Without  this, you'll have to code and try&nbsp;
to implement these parts yourself.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.660
Lastly, analytics.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:31.811
I love these because it helps you get to know how&nbsp;
much your APIs are being used.

00:06:34.046 --> 00:06:37.473
And once you have users subscribe,

00:06:37.473 --> 00:06:43.599
you have this in place, I'll be&nbsp;able to know
who are my most important users of my APIs. 

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:49.120
 
Without API management here you really&nbsp;
don't have a strong indicator of how much people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:54.560
are using, how many errors you're getting back,&nbsp;
what's the performance, what's the latency. 

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.558
So,  all these decisions are really hard to - you're&nbsp;
kind of guessing that as well.

00:06:58.691 --> 00:07:04.641
Now for you, the organization making the APIs, 
one of my best&nbsp;things here as well is:

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:09.207
what do we work on?

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:19.113
In today's culture we are a data-driven society,&nbsp;
and this will clearly give us the metrics to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:25.120
we need to devote our time to this particular API&nbsp;
set , because this we have the data to back it up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.546
that this is what people want, is what they're&nbsp;
using here as well.

00:07:29.546 --> 00:07:32.189
So, with this, let's to recap here. 

00:07:32.189 --> 00:07:37.493
API management is important for the&nbsp;
mere fact of the flexibility it gives you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.493 --> 00:07:42.472
your architecture, the security options you have&nbsp;
at your fingertips to implement

00:07:42.472 --> 00:07:45.191
and, also, the analytics you get back.

